Low contamination by CO2 was demonstrated by XRD analysis of the annealed samples in Fig. 6
                     : XRD of samples BCO_4, annealed at 800 °C and 1000 °C, showing BaCeO3 as the majority phase, with CeO2 found in both samples annealed at 800 °C and 1000 °C. The sample annealed at 800 °C additionally showed that a low amount of BaCO3 is also present, which is consistent with the decomposition temperatures observed in Fig. 5. Rietveld analysis of the sample BCO_4_1000, annealed at 1000 °C, provided the CeO2 content at 18%. Raman spectroscopy was then performed (Fig. 7
                     ), which confirmed the presence of bands characteristic to BaCO3 in the sample annealed at 800 °C. These characteristic bands were, however, absent from the sample annealed at 1000 °C, suggesting that BaCO3 was eliminated during the annealing.

To attempt to determine the particle size of prepared powders, multiple methods were employed. Laser diffraction measurements were obtained from samples with a greater degree of BaCeO3 content (BCO_3 and BCO_4), where the results (Fig. 8
                     ) showed a particle size in the ranges of 5–82 µm, with median values for particle size well above 20 µm. These measurements are heavily contradictory to values obtained by Rietveld refinement of XRD spectra, which suggested that peak widening characteristic of nanoparticles was occurring. Similarly, TEM imaging (Figs. 9–11


                     ) shows a significantly different particle size, with observed particles ranging from tens of nanometers to just over a micrometer in diameter. This large difference in the LD measurement is most likely the result of agglomeration, which is not uncommon for nanoparticles, and can be observed in the TEM imaging (Fig. 11). This presents a significant difficulty in ascertaining the actual median size of the obtained crystallites.
